@model ReformedHistory.Views.Content.Confessions.Secret.SecretSection

<h2 id="@this.Model.Link">Chapter @this.Model.Index</h2>
<h3>@this.Model.Name</h3>
<br />

<div class="centerQuote">
    <span>
        For anyone who has neglected these things and has sought false gods, who has lived 
        shamefully or impiously, and who has worshiped God more with ceremonies or external 
        things than with true holiness of life, will be excluded, disinherited, and rejected
        from the covenant
    </span>
    <span>Heinrich Bullinger, The One and Eternal Testament or Covenant of God</span>
</div>
    
<ol>
    @*<li>
        All the benefits of being in union with Christ are secured and received by faith alone, including justification, 
        sanctification, adoption, and participation in the sacraments.
    </li>*@
    <li>
        If a Christian cultivates unbelief in their heart by resisting the Holy Spirit,
        by having a persistent lack of repentance, by failing to grow in the grace given to them in baptism, 
        and by allying themselves with 
        the sins of the flesh against the power of the Spirit, they destroy in themselves the only faculty
        that may receive Christ unto salvation; that is, they destroy faith.
    </li>
    <li>
        If faith is destroyed, a person is cut off from Christ and cannot be saved.
    </li>
    <li>
        This does not mean that promises of God are revoked, 
        for God remains faithful even if his people are faithless. However, if the means by which God's people receive 
        His promises are lost, so too are the benefits of those promises lost. The scriptures testify
        to this loss of faith by calling it the mortal sin, blasphemy of the Holy Spirit, 
        recrucifying the Christ, and other such names.
    </li>
    <li>
        A believer does not apostatize through works, as if they must sustain the grace of God through their 
        own effort. Every sin can be forgiven by Christ through faith and repentance, except
        the loss of faith and repentance.
    </li>
    <li>
        Apostasy is final, for it is impossible to restore a person to repentance who has
        known the way of salvation and abandoned it.
    </li>
    <li>
        Nevertheless, is most unwise to hastily judge anyone as finally apostate or reprobate, so that the gospel is not preached and
        repentance is not demanded. The state of any man's soul is a mystery known only to God, and there are many
        whose faith hangs by a thread with little outward fruit. Such people are in need of the gospel most of all,
        and so we must preach even to those who appear to have abandoned the faith.
    </li>
</ol>
<br />